{
  "_about": "How much each background moves at each energy, measured on 2026-11-11. One clip per background, page and energy: the composition bg-<name>, 4 seconds at 60 frames a second, brand tulvo, with the sample headline and card in the middle, rendered at half size (--scale=0.5 --crf=22) and measured with tools/study/profile.py and tools/checks/smoothness.py. Paper is measured on the light page only and Night on the dark page only: those are the pages they are made for.",
  "_how_to_read": "profile.py scores each quarter-second: under 0.15 is still, and a film's typical movement is about 1. share_of_time_moving is the share of quarter-seconds at or over 0.15; typical_movement is the middle score of those (0 when there are none); peak_movement is the highest quarter-second; mean_movement is the average over the whole clip, the fairest single figure for a background because most never reach 0.15.",
  "_what_it_shows": [
    "At rest every background measures as still (share_of_time_moving 0, highest quarter-second 0.08 or less), on both pages.",
    "Colour field on the light page used to read 0.17 to 0.24 at every energy because of the Stage's grain, which is set for a near-white page. Since 11 Nov (evening) Colour field takes a fifth of the light page's grain (useGrainShare in engine/src/kit/ui.tsx); its light-page figures were measured again after that change.",
    "At alive and peak the movement is real and ordered (mean_movement rises from rest to alive to peak, by two to five times in all for most; less where the page's grain is most of the figure), but for almost every background it stays under profile.py's 0.15 line. That line needs the average pixel to change about four and a half levels of 255 every second; a soft, low-contrast drift would have to turn over every few seconds to reach it, which would no longer be a slow drift. Only Waves and Glass on the dark page touch the line at peak. So a hold on any of these backgrounds, at any energy, still counts as rest in a film's profile.",
    "Blueprint reads the same at alive and at peak: the only thing that moves is the head of one thin path.",
    "No clip has a jump or a judder window. The reel has no jumps other than its 21 cuts and no judder windows, for tulvo and for demo, at rest, alive and peak."
  ],
  "_tools": "Every figure is what tools/study/profile.py and tools/checks/smoothness.py print as they stand. (Earlier on the same day profile.py misread a frame difference that ffmpeg prints as 6.94e-05 as 6.94, which showed false jumps on still clips; it was corrected at 18:20, and these figures were taken after that.)",
  "measured_on": "2026-11-11",
  "brand": "tulvo",
  "stillness_line": 0.15,
